# Architecture

## Background content

<dfn>Background content</dfn> is code that runs independently of any web page, browser window, or extension page. It responds to events from the extension APIs and the user agent, and it can use the extension APIs that the extension has permission for. It runs in a [=privileged extension context=]. It is event-based: the user agent loads it when an event occurs and may unload it when it is idle, as described in [[#background-lifecycle]]. The extension declares it in the [[#key-background]] manifest key.

The user agent runs background content in one of two environments:

* A <dfn>service worker environment</dfn>, in which the user agent runs a [=service worker=] from the [=extension origin=].
* A <dfn>document environment</dfn>, in which the user agent loads an [=HTML document=] from the [=extension origin=] without displaying it.

A user agent supports the [=service worker environment=], the [=document environment=], or both.

When the user agent loads an extension that has a `background` key, it runs the steps to [=select the background environment=] on the value of that key. The steps return a <dfn>background selection</dfn>, a pair of an environment and a key of the `background` object, or null. The key is the one whose value determines the resource: `service_worker`, `page`, or `scripts`.

The user agent runs the resource of a [=background selection=] in the environment of that selection, as described in [[#resource-of-a-background-environment]]. When the steps return null, the extension has no background content.

Background content can fail to load, for example when its resource cannot be fetched or a script throws as it first runs. A [=document environment=] loads its resource as any [=HTML document=] loads, and a [=service worker environment=] loads its resource as any [=service worker=] script loads, so the default behavior of each applies. An API that expects background content can fail when the content did not load. A failure to load the resource must not by itself prevent the extension from loading, and the user agent must not select a second environment. The user agent may [=report an error=].

Issue(#1156): Specify what counts as a failure to load, such as a resource that cannot be fetched or a script that throws while it first runs. Also specify how a failure affects the other scripts of a [=background selection=] that uses [[#key-background-scripts]]. In the [=generated service worker=], the scripts load one after another, so a script that cannot be fetched can prevent the scripts after it from loading. In the [=generated background document=], each <{script}> element loads on its own, so the scripts after a failed one still run. The two generated resources are expected to fail in the same way.

### Resource of a background environment

The key and the environment of a [=background selection=] determine the resource that the user agent runs:

* For [[#key-background-service_worker]], the environment is a [=service worker environment=], and the resource is the JavaScript file at the [=resolved path=] of the value. The file is run as a [=module script=] when [[#key-background-type]] is `"module"`, and as a [=classic script=] otherwise.
* For [[#key-background-page]], the environment is a [=document environment=], and the resource is the [=HTML document=] at the [=resolved path=] of the value. [[#key-background-type]] does not affect the document.
* For [[#key-background-scripts]], the environment is either one, and the resource depends on it:
    * In a [=service worker environment=], the resource is the [=generated service worker=].
    * In a [=document environment=], the resource is the [=generated background document=].

The <dfn>generated service worker</dfn> is a JavaScript file in the [=extension origin=], at the path `/_generated_service_worker.js`, whose filename is [reserved](#reserved-file-names). It loads the [=resolved path=] of each path in [[#key-background-scripts]], in order. When [[#key-background-type]] is `"module"`, it imports each path as a [=module script=]. Otherwise, it loads the paths as [=classic scripts=] with {{WorkerGlobalScope/importScripts()}}. All of the scripts run in the same [=global object=].

Issue(#785): The [=generated service worker=] is not implemented by all user agents.

The <dfn>generated background document</dfn> is an [=HTML document=] in the [=extension origin=], at the path `/_generated_background_page.html`, whose filename is [reserved](#reserved-file-names). It contains one <{script}> element for each path in [[#key-background-scripts]], in order, with the [=resolved path=] of that path as its `src`. Each element is a [=module script=] when [[#key-background-type]] is `"module"`, and a [=classic script=] otherwise. All of the scripts run in the same [=global object=].

### Declaring several environments

[[#key-background-service_worker]] may be present together with [[#key-background-scripts]] or with [[#key-background-page]], so that one manifest works in user agents that support different environments. `scripts` and `page` are alternatives, and the user agent may [=report a warning=] when both are present. When two keys provide a resource for the same environment, `service_worker` and `page` take precedence over `scripts`. The [[#key-background-preferred_environment]] key lets the extension choose between the environments that a user agent supports.

A user agent must ignore the keys that belong only to environments it does not support, even when their values would otherwise be [=invalid=], and may [=report a warning=] for them.

### Background lifecycle

[=Background content=] is event-based. The user agent loads it when an event occurs for which it has registered a listener, and may unload it when it is idle. Unloading discards the [=global object=] of the environment and the state held in it. A later event loads the background content again.

Issue(#1158): Define when a listener counts as registered. The user agent has to observe the listeners that the background content adds while it loads, and keep them so that a later event can load the background content again. A proposal adds a method that lets the extension tell the user agent it has finished registering listeners. See [Asynchronous Listener Registration](https://github.com/w3c/webextensions/blob/main/proposals/async-listener-registration.md).

Issue: Specify when the background content first loads, what counts as idle, and how events that arrive while it is loading are ordered. See <#147>, <#416>, and <#464>.

## <dfn>Content scripts</dfn>

Content scripts represent a set of JS and CSS files that should be injected into matching pages loaded by the user agent. They are injected using the steps in [[#inject-a-content-script]]. Content scripts run in an [=isolated world=] by default.

### Key `matches` ### {#key-content_scripts-matches}

A [=list=] of [=match patterns=] that are used to decide which pages the user agent injects the content script into. This key is required.

### Key `exclude_matches`

A [=list=] of [=match patterns=] that can be used to specify URLs where the content script should not run, even if the URL matches entries in [[#key-content_scripts-matches]] and (if specified) [[#key-include_globs]].

### Key `js`

A [=list=] of [=package paths=] of JavaScript files that should be injected as scripts. A value that contains an item that is not a [=package path=] is [=invalid=].

### Key `css`

A [=list=] of [=package paths=] of CSS files that should be injected as stylesheets. A value that contains an item that is not a [=package path=] is [=invalid=].

### Key `all_frames`

If `all_frames` is `true`, the content script must be injected into any subframes that match the other matching criteria for the content script. If `false`, content scripts will only be injected into top-level documents. Defaults to `false`.

### Key `match_about_blank`

If this is `true`, use the URL of the parent frame when matching a child frame whose document URL is `about:blank` or `about:srcdoc`. See also [[#determine-the-url-for-matching-a-document]]. Defaults to `false`.

### Key `match_origin_as_fallback`

If this is `true`, use fallbacks as described in [[#determine-the-url-for-matching-a-document]].

No path is available when the URL to match against falls back to an origin. Therefore, when set, the user agent may treat a [[#key-content_scripts-matches]] with a path other than `/*` as an error.

Defaults to `false`.

### Key `run_at`

Specifies when the content script should be injected. Valid values are defined by the {{RunAt}} enum.

### Key `include_globs`

A list of [=globs=] that a document should match. A document matches if the URL matches both the [[#key-content_scripts-matches]] field and the [[#key-include_globs]] field.

### Key `exclude_globs`

A [=list=] of [=globs=] that can be used to specify URLs where the content script should not run, even if the URL matches entries in [[#key-content_scripts-matches]] and (if specified) [[#key-include_globs]].

### Key `world`

The [=world=] any JavaScript scripts should be injected into. Defaults to `ISOLATED`. Valid values are defined by the {{ExecutionWorld}} enum.

### <dfn>RunAt</dfn> enum

<pre class="idl">
enum RunAt {
    "document_start",
    "document_end",
    "document_idle"
};
</pre>

The {{RunAt}} enum represents when a content script should be injected.

### <dfn>ExecutionWorld</dfn> enum

<pre class="idl">
enum ExecutionWorld {
    "ISOLATED",
    "MAIN"
};
</pre>

The {{ExecutionWorld}} enum represents a JavaScript [=world=].

"ISOLATED" corresponds to an [=isolated world=].

"MAIN" corresponds to the [=main world=].

